    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "a931e54a_66ec62ce",
        "filename": "drivers/of/platform.c",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 61,
      "author": {
        "id": 1446383
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-05-22T15:50:34Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e you can\u0027t change a pointer in an unatomic way on Linux.\nIs this true on a 64-bit machine? Specifically ARM64? I\u0027m not sure if the bus supports a 64-bit atomic read/write. I don\u0027t think it\u0027s a guaranteed standard and might be implementation defined. I agree on a 32-bit machine, it is atomic (at least on ARM32/64).\n\nEven if the pointer update itself is atomic, I\u0027m using the lock here because I need to keep the pointer update (device being freed) atomic with device reference being grabbed. I know get_device() does NULL check, but there\u0027s nothing preventing the dev memory from get freed (say the last reference does a put_device()) after the NULL check. An interrupt could come in right after the NULL check, some other thread puts the last reference and the memory is freed. Now, the \"dev\" used by get_device() would be doing a use after free. Wouldn\u0027t it?",
      "parentUuid": "fcfcccfd_ea243c8d",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 61,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 61,
        "endChar": 27
      },
      "revId": "e43ba023452692e322cf38f022b467757f12997c",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "9ba245ef_d75fdee2",
        "filename": "drivers/of/platform.c",
